A-19: predictive back silently disabled the back-key handling. API 36 emulator, 2026-11-02.

## What is wrong

CaptureActivity and HelpActivity handled the back key in onKeyDown. From API 33 the back
key stops being delivered there once an app opts into OnBackInvokedDispatcher -- and for an
app targeting API 36, which this one does, that opt-in is the default. So both handlers
stopped running and nobody noticed, because the system's default back does something
plausible: it leaves the activity.

The visible symptom: **with a scan result on screen, back left the app** instead of
returning to the preview. On a scanner whose whole loop is scan -> look -> scan again, that
is the back button doing the opposite of what it did for fifteen years.

## Reproduced, and the cause confirmed by A/B

API 36 emulator, virtual-scene camera, a QR decoded off the poster so a result is on screen
(`a19-predictive-back-result-on-screen-api36.png`), then a real back gesture:

| | back with a result on screen |
| --- | --- |
| before the fix | **leaves the app** -> NexusLauncherActivity |
| before the fix, with `android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="false"` | stays, preview restarts |
| after the fix | stays, preview restarts |

The middle row is the cause, isolated: opting out of predictive back restores the old
behaviour exactly, which no other explanation does. The flag was set only for that
measurement and is not in the tree -- opting out is not the fix, it is the diagnosis.

## A trap that nearly produced the wrong conclusion

**`adb shell input keyevent KEYCODE_BACK` is not a back press** under predictive back. The
first reproduction used it, and after the fix it still appeared to leave the app -- while
the callback demonstrably ran, which the log line proved. An injected key event reaches both
the dispatcher and the legacy path, so the handler runs *and* the activity finishes.

A real edge-swipe gesture behaves correctly in both directions. Every result above is from
a gesture. The pre-fix reproduction was re-run with a gesture before any of this was
believed, because a fix verified with the wrong input is not verified.

## After the fix

| case | expected | observed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| result on screen, source NONE | return to the preview | returns, and immediately rescans the poster |
| no result on screen | leave the app | leaves |
| HelpActivity, no web history | leave Help | returns to CaptureActivity |

Android 14 (DN-ENG41): no crash, and the 100-cycle open/close loop is clean -- 0 fatals,
0 camera errors.

Not tested: the NATIVE_APP_INTENT path, which needs a caller app. It is the one case the
fix deliberately leaves to the system -- finishing without setResult already answers
RESULT_CANCELED, which is exactly what the removed branch did explicitly.
